    "author_association": "NONE",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nWhen `showcmdloc` is set to `statusline`, `%S` can display corrupted contents (something like `~@<fd>`) after calling `:redraw` from a keymap callback, even if `statusline` is left default.\n\n### Additional observations\n\n- `showcmdloc=statusline`: `%S` displays corrupted contents.\n- `showcmdloc=tabline`: `%S` displays different corrupted contents (`~@` followed by an invalid character).\n- `showcmdloc=last`: nothing is displayed.\n\nThe issue reproduces on the latest `master` in a `Release` build.\nIn a `Debug` build, `%S` never displays anything instead of showing corrupted contents.\n\nInitially this appeared while using `which-key.nvim`, but it can be reproduced without any plugins using the minimal configuration above, so it appears to be an issue in Neovim itself rather than in `which-key`.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```lua\nvim.o.showcmdloc = \"statusline\"\nvim.o.statusline = \"%S\"\nvim.keymap.set('n', 'm', function()\n  -- vim.cmd.redraw()\n  vim.api.nvim__redraw {\n    statusline = true,\n  }\nend, {})\n```\nUse this minimum reproduction config, and press `m` in nvim\n\n### Expected behavior\n\n`%S` correctly shows which key is pressed.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\nNVIM v0.12.4\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nvim 9.2.0735 has similar problem\n\n### Operating system/version\n\narchlinux\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.47.4\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\narchlinux repository",
